<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vagad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vagad is a hilly, forested region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, roughly covering the modern districts of Dungarpur and Banswara. It has a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> population and its own dialect, Wagdi. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was ruled by branches of the Guhila family of Mewar, whose principalities of Dungarpur and Banswara survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> until 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vagad.jpg" alt="Vagad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaisingh rathore at English Wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dev_Somnath_Temple,_Dungarpur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Scholars have called the region by several names, among them Bagar, Vagwar, Vaiyagad, Vagat, Varget and Bagad. It is bounded on the north by Mewar, on the east and south-east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and on the west and south-west by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Most of it lies in the upper watershed of the Mahi River and its tributaries, said to be the lifeline of Vagad. The Mahi rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhya-range">Vindhya Range</a>, flows north through Banswara, then turns south-west to form the boundary between Banswara and Dungarpur before entering Gujarat and reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Cambay</a>.</p><p>Its forests are mainly of teak, and its wildlife includes the leopard and the chinkara. Besides Dungarpur and Banswara, its towns include Aspur, Bhiluda, Simalwada, Sagwara, Partapur, Bagidra and Garhi. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> counted 3,186,037 people in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the thirteenth century much of Vagad is said to have been held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhils</a> and the rest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> and Parmars. Mahap, the eldest son of Karan Singh, the Rawal of Chittor, was forced to leave his father's state and went to the part of Vagad held by the Chauhans, his mother's family, with a band of Bhils of the Damor section. With Chauhan support he extended his rule over the Bhils of Vagad, carved out a state and took the title of Rawal.</p><p>As the elder son, Mahap kept the surname Ahada, which his ancestors had adopted in place of Guhilot, while his younger brother's line took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> and the title of Rana. In 1357 one of Mahap's successors, Bir Singh, killed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> chief named Dungaria, took his territory and founded Dungarpur on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vagad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Maharawals of Dungarpur claimed descent from the elder line of the Guhila dynasty of Mewar, said to have moved south into the <b>Vagad</b> hills in the late 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/dungarpur-state">Dungarpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>Kushalgarh was held by a branch of Rathore Rajputs who settled in the hills of <b>Vagad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kushalgarh-state">Kushalgarh State</a></li></ul></section>
